# Suggest hardening (ALGR)

**Harden** = take something that already works on the happy path and make it **reliable under real use** (edges, retries, caps, tests, stale canon, dual-notify, empty states).

When shipping or reviewing work, **suggest** hardening if it would improve **quality** or **best practices** — do not wait to be asked.

## When to suggest

- New or changed behavior that works only on the main path
- Missing caps, coalesce, idle, idempotency, or skip reasons
- Docs/UI still describing old timing or copy
- Immediate alerts that also queue a digest (or other duplicate sends)
- Tests that do not lock the new invariant
- Accessibility, data integrity, or ops gaps that are not already a hard stop

## How

1. Name the gap in one or two sentences and the harden (what would change).
2. **Ask** before doing extra hardening unless the user already said to harden this work.
3. Keep the suggestion short; do not turn every tweak into a program.

## Do not

- Silently expand scope with unrequested hardening
- Repeat the same suggestion after they defer or reject it
- Duplicate **challenge-best-practices** (that rule **stops** violating approaches). This rule **offers** reliability work when the request itself is valid.

Pairs with **harden-audit** (full-repo suggestion list on request), **ask-before-building**, and **idiomatic-methodology**.
